there's various difficulties to working  and filming news in Gaza the first the  obvious one is security and danger Gaza  is one big frontline  there's shells dropping out of the sky  on a regular basis this tank parked up  around the borders so you know you're  operating within a within dangerous area  where things could go wrong and you can  get injured  well you're wearing a flak jacket you're  wearing a helmet which are heavy it's 30  plus degrees you have a camera the  weighs in excess of 10 kilos so you know  getting in now the car is it's hard work  running around chasing stuff it's hard  work I have to be aware of and I have a  sensibility towards the cultural  differences if you get that wrong people  get very angry and they'll make that  very clear to you there's some things I  could film but I choose not to because I  know they're never going to be seen  they're too appalling  [Music]  the most emotional shots are obviously  of people there's a young child who'd  been injured and quite badly and as he  went I saw at the corner of my eye his  mother was sitting there watching for  the go and so we got to see his mother  sort of sitting there watching her child  leave to go to the operating theatre and  that's you know it's it's it stays with  you seeing the kid disappear through the  door and the mother sitting there I  think a little you know I don't know  what happens next  we found this family whose three-story  house has been largely destroyed and we  actually went up onto the third floor to  interview the husband and wife and the  shot we got shows in the background this  sort of what looks like a corridor has  been ripped through this neighborhood  and the lady is standing there we're up  high and the elevation works and just  it's just utter destruction behind her  it was a shot that reminded me of the  sort of things I'd seen as a kid of her  Oshima where entire city force of the  living in the particular angle in the  particular framing lent itself to that  idea of just utter destruction the shot  that I got that gives you some idea of  the destructive power of some of these  shells the landing  you can actually see the camera wobbling  although that landed a good couple  hundred meters away from the hotel the  blast is such that the camera wobbled I  think anyone coming here would be  affected by the things you see  particularly injured and killed having  two children of my own obviously adds  another dimension to that you know when  I see injured and their children you  know sometimes I do think well my  goodness like that could be my son that  could be my daughter but the point  you're actually filming and the the  viewfinder is to your eye I find that I  tend to sort of disassociate myself from  others around me and concentrate on  doing the best job I can on a technical  level as soon as the camera comes off  your shoulder though you're surrounded  by that story again by those people by  their relatives by their destroyed house  and you have to re-engage you can't you  can't operate here just as a sort of  machine just picking up shots you have  to engage with it
 

